\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The real algebra generated by the Dirac gamma matrices}

\p{On #{\mathbb R^4}, let

##{q_\gamma(v)=v_0^2-v_1^2-v_2^2-v_3^2.}

Under the coordinate identification
#{\operatorname{Fin}(4)=\operatorname{Fin}(1+3)}, the identity map is an
isometry from #{q_\gamma} to the signature form #{Q_{1,3}} of
\ref{fcap-0026}. This is equality of the displayed forms, rather than only
equality of their signatures. Thus the Clifford transport of
\ref{fcap-0029} and the classification in \ref{fcap-0026} give

##{\Cl(q_\gamma)\simeq\Cl_{1,3}\simeq M_2(\mathbb H)}

as real algebras. The selected formal ingredients are the coordinate formula
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordForm_apply} and the residue-two
classification
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realClifford_classification_of_residue_eq_two};
the latter supplies existence of an abstract equivalence, not a theorem
about a particular gamma-matrix map.}

\p{Let #{\xi:\operatorname{Fin}(1)\sqcup\operatorname{Fin}(3)
\simeq\operatorname{Fin}(4)} be the standard index equivalence, and write

##{\Gamma_i=\operatorname{endEquivMatrix}
\bigl(\operatorname{gamma}(\xi^{-1}i)\bigr).}

These are the concrete matrices #{\gamma^0,\gamma^1,\gamma^2,\gamma^3};
their squares are #{+1,-1,-1,-1}, and distinct matrices anticommute. Put

##{D_\gamma=\operatorname{Alg}_{\mathbb R}
\langle\Gamma_0,\Gamma_1,\Gamma_2,\Gamma_3\rangle
\subset M_4(\mathbb C).}

Physlib constructs the real unital algebra homomorphism

##{\rho:\Cl(q_\gamma)\longrightarrow D_\gamma,\qquad
\rho\bigl(\iota_{q_\gamma}(v)\bigr)=\sum_{i=0}^3v_i\Gamma_i}

from the Clifford universal property and proves that it is surjective onto
#{D_\gamma}; see the
\href{https://github.com/leanprover-community/physlib/blob/44c66d54be78db4693be9f8f92bd3b5ad124ed6f/Physlib/Relativity/CliffordAlgebra.lean#L98-L156}{generator-map source}
and the
\href{https://github.com/leanprover-community/physlib/blob/44c66d54be78db4693be9f8f92bd3b5ad124ed6f/Physlib/Relativity/CliffordAlgebra.lean#L158-L207}{surjectivity source}.
The codomain here is the generated real subalgebra, not all of
#{M_4(\mathbb C)}.}

\p{It remains to prove that this actual map has zero kernel. First,
#{M_2(\mathbb H)} is simple. Let #{J} be a nonzero two-sided ideal, choose a
matrix #{A\in J} with nonzero entry #{a=A_{ij}}, and let #{E_{kl}(b)} denote
the matrix whose only nonzero entry is #{b} in position #{(k,l)}. Since every
nonzero quaternion is invertible, for all #{k,l} one has

##{E_{ki}(a^{-1})\,A\,E_{jl}(1)=E_{kl}(1)\in J.}

The order of the factors matters over #{\mathbb H}. Taking both diagonal
matrix units and adding them gives #{1\in J}, so #{J=M_2(\mathbb H)}.
Transport through the preceding equivalence makes #{\Cl(q_\gamma)} simple.
Hence #{\ker\rho} is zero or the entire source. The second option is
impossible because #{\rho} is unital and the identity matrix in
#{D_\gamma\subset M_4(\mathbb C)} is nonzero. Therefore #{\rho} is
injective; together with the formal surjectivity, this proves that this same
map is a real algebra equivalence

##{\Cl(q_\gamma)\simeq_{\rho}D_\gamma.}}

\p{Consequently

##{\dim_{\mathbb R}D_\gamma
=\dim_{\mathbb R}M_2(\mathbb H)=4\cdot4=16.}

For the ambient algebra,

##{\dim_{\mathbb R}M_4(\mathbb C)=16\cdot2=32.}

Thus #{D_\gamma} is a proper real subalgebra of the ambient complex matrix
algebra. This conclusion uses the proved equivalence and does not assume the
image dimension in order to deduce injectivity.}

\p{Lawson--Michelsohn invoke simplicity of #{M_n(K)} for
#{K=\mathbb R,\mathbb C,\mathbb H} in their real representation theorem
\citet{I.5, Theorem 5.6, pp. 31--32}{lawson2016spin}. The matrix-unit
calculation above supplies that step for the quaternionic algebra and then
applies it to Physlib's concrete homomorphism. Their convention is
#{v^2=-q(v)1}, so our #{\Cl_{1,3}} is their #{\Cl_{3,1}}. Their algebra in
that convention is the quaternionic matrix algebra used in the simplicity
argument. The abstract classification is formalized in Tau Ceti, and
Physlib formalizes the concrete map and its surjectivity. The simplicity and
kernel argument completing the equivalence here is informal; no whole-card
formal badge is asserted.}
